          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When one code already covers both a condition and its manifestation, is it wrong to add a second code for the manifestation?",
          "answer": "The short answer from the retrieved passages is that it depends on the instruction attached to the code, not on a general rule.\n\nThe ICD-10-CM conventions summary says a combination code is used instead of separate codes where one exists. It also describes \"code first\" and \"use additional code\" as a pair that sets the order of reporting, and \"code also\" as a signal that two codes are needed. (Source: ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions.) So if the classification's instructions call for a second code, adding it is correct, and leaving it out would be the error.\n\nThe diabetes passage gives examples of this. Some diabetes codes still need a second code. Diabetic chronic kidney disease is paired with a code for the stage of kidney disease, and a diabetic foot ulcer is paired with a code for its site and depth. (Source: Coding type 2 diabetes, E11.) The asthma passage shows the same pattern in a different setting: where both asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease are documented, the classification asks for an asthma code to be added, so both are reported. (Source: Coding asthma, J45.)\n\nThe passages do not give a general rule that a second code for a manifestation is always wrong or always right. They also do not cover the specific code pair you have in mind. If you tell me which two codes or which condition you mean, I can check what the retrieved material says about that case, though I can't confirm it against the current official tables. Coding decisions for real claims or records should be checked with a trained medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM tabular list and its guidelines.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
